          "prompt": "Stylized hand-painted 3D game environment art for a fast first-person roguelite shooter, matching the\nreadable visual direction of Gunfire Reborn: bold chunky silhouettes, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant\ncolour blocks, soft painted gradients and a few restrained edge accents. East-Asian underground forge-palace fantasy of Zhurong, the Chinese god of fire: massive black iron (xuan tie) with heavy rivets, dark charcoal basalt blocks, deep vermilion lacquer, warm aged bronze ding cauldrons, chunky thick iron chains always attached flat to the surfaces, soot-darkened edges, and a few channels and cracks of molten lava. Lava, embers and fire are painted as opaque flat bright orange-yellow surfaces, never as smoke, loose sparks or transparent effects. Matte stone,\nwood and cloth, restrained highlights on metal, sparse painted wear. No photorealism, micro-grain, dense tiny cracks,\nelaborate miniature carvings, harsh specular highlights or baked cast shadows. Even neutral lighting.\n\nAsset (10m wide x 11m tall x 5m deep): A monumental forge-palace shrine niche 10m wide, 11m tall, 5m deep that frames a magic portal: a thick facade of dark basalt and riveted black iron with ONE tall open round-topped arch in the middle, 6.5m wide and 8m tall, leading into a shallow open recess 3.5m deep whose floor is flat at ground level (no steps or objects inside the recess). The recess back wall carries a large carved bronze fire-sun disc. Two vermilion lacquer pillars flank the arch, each topped by a small bronze fire bowl with glowing coals; a narrow lava groove glowing molten orange outlines the arch; the facade is crowned by a dark bronze tiled roof with upturned eaves. The arch is empty, nothing inside the opening.\n\nOutput: one orthographic model turnaround sheet for 3D reconstruction. Exactly four separate complete views\nof the SAME object in one row, left to right:\n1. FRONT view: the object's front faces the viewer.\n2. LEFT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image (camera at the\n   object's right-hand side).\n3. BACK view: the object's back faces the viewer.\n4. RIGHT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image.\nSame scale and same ground line in all four, each centred in its quarter with generous empty margin, never touching or\noverlapping; the whole object fits inside its quarter, nothing cropped.\nPlain flat light neutral grey background (#D6D6D6). No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no\nperspective.",
